Acne Vulgaris
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Comedones/Papules/Pustules, Seborrhoea, Facial/Truncal distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-002
Reason for Visit: Acute secondary excoriation and bleeding of facial acne lesions resulting in severe distress and localized pain.
Location: Residential group home
Present at Visit: Patient, Support Worker
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with an intellectual disability who was highly agitated, actively picking at painful facial lesions, and bleeding persistently. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate the severity of the lesions, rule out superinfection, achieve hemostasis, and provide immediate symptom relief to prevent further self-harm and deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "It hurts and itches so much, I can't stop scratching it. Look at the blood."
History of Present Illness: Over the past 72 hours, the patient has experienced an acute exacerbation of facial and truncal acne. The lesions have become highly pruritic and tender. Over the last 6 hours, the patient has been continuously excoriating the erythematous papules and pustules, leading to post-inflammatory erythema, raw bleeding surfaces, and significant psychosocial distress. The support worker notes the patient has increased sebum production with very oily skin. The pain and tenderness associated with the nodular lesions have completely disrupted the patient's evening routine and sleep pattern.
Functional Status: Requires support for complex daily tasks, independent in basic mobility.
Social/Safety Concerns: Risk of ongoing self-excoriation leading to severe secondary infection and scarring; caregiver distress managing the acute agitation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 102 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Agitated, pacing the room, hands frequently touching the face.
Physical Exam: The face and upper trunk exhibit widespread seborrhoea. There is a classic distribution of open and closed comedones, alongside multiple erythematous papules and pustules. Several nodular lesions on the jawline display fresh excoriations, pinpoint bleeding, and focal serosanguinous weeping. No extensive surrounding induration, fluctuant abscess formation, or lymphangitic streaking.
Environmental Observations: Appropriate disability accommodations in place. No sharps accessible to the patient.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current active prescriptions for acne.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acne Vulgaris with acute excoriation and behavioral distress - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Impetigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of classic honey-colored crusts; lesions clearly originate from underlying comedones.

Eczema Herpeticum - Ruled out/unlikely due to: No clustered, monomorphic, punched-out erosions; patient is afebrile with no systemic illness.

Delusional Parasitosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear objective evidence of primary inflammatory acne driving the pruritus and picking behavior.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute behavioral and clinical crisis driven by painful, pruritic acne vulgaris. The continuous self-excoriation has resulted in bleeding, raw dermal exposure, and a high risk of imminent secondary staphylococcal infection. Triage correctly identified the bleeding, pain, and escalating agitation as requiring urgent medical intervention. Immediate after-hours assessment was essential to perform wound care, rule out existing deep tissue infection, and initiate topical treatments and oral antihistamines to break the itch-scratch cycle. Delaying this assessment until morning would have inevitably resulted in further self-inflicted skin trauma, worsening agitation, and a higher likelihood of requiring hospital admission for systemic antibiotics.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Cleansed excoriated areas with saline. Applied hydrocolloid patches to bleeding/raw nodules. Prescribed oral Cetirizine 10mg stat and daily for pruritus. Recommended gentle skin cleansers.
Positive Behavioral Change: By wearing the soft cotton gloves provided by your support worker tonight, you will protect your skin from sharp fingernails, which will allow the raw spots to heal faster and stop the stinging pain. Placing the hydrocolloid patches over the sore spots will create a protective barrier, reducing the urge to pick and promoting rapid, painless healing overnight.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Instructed support staff on daily wound care.
DME/Safety (Optional): Soft cotton gloves for nighttime use.
Follow-up: Regular GP review in 3-5 days to monitor healing and establish a long-term acne management plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops a fever, the facial redness begins spreading rapidly, the skin becomes hot to the touch, or if agitation becomes unmanageable by staff, present to the nearest Emergency Department or call 000.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is based on clinical presentation today. Open wounds carry an ongoing risk of infection.
Clinical Course: The acute bleeding and stinging should resolve within 24-48 hours with barrier protection. The underlying acne requires ongoing medical management.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Support staff must monitor for increasing heat, swelling, or spreading redness around the sores, or if the patient develops a temperature above 38.0C, which requires immediate medical review.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The antihistamine will reduce the deep itching sensation, and the patches physically protect the skin from further damage, allowing the top layer to regenerate safely.
Actionable Steps: Leave the protective patches in place until they fall off naturally. Take the anti-itch medication every evening to ensure a restful, scratch-free sleep.

